/* std/thread: the thread surface. Stage A2 of MK9-S3-M1 added the fence, stage
   B1 added the checker rules for `spawn` and `join` (below), and stage C1
   lowered the two on both backends.

       fun fence(order: Ordering)
       fun spawn<t, r>(entry: closure fun(move t) => r, arg: move t) => Thread<r>
       fun join<t>(handle: move Thread<t>) => Result<t, ThreadErr>

   `spawn` and `join` are *checker* builtins: the compiler builds their
   signatures at each call site, because what crosses the boundary decides the
   types, and stage C1 lowers them through the pthread machinery in
   `runtime/kelp_rt.c` plus one generated worker body per call signature. Only
   `ThreadErr` is declared here, and the two calls are documented rather than
   declared for the reason the fence is: their signatures are generic in the
   values that cross the thread boundary and there is no `.kp` body to write.

       fun thread.spawn(entry, arg)

   `entry` is a declared `fun`, a `fun` literal, or a capturing closure, and it
   takes exactly one parameter: the value `arg` is moved into the worker as that
   parameter. A plain function value is widened where a closure is expected, so
   all three spellings work. `arg` is moved, never borrowed, because a borrow's
   lifetime in v1 is lexical in the frame that made it and the worker outlives
   that frame.

   One exception, and it is the whole of v1's thread-shared data: a *shared*
   borrow may cross when the place it borrows has process lifetime, which in v1
   means a `pd` global, and when the borrowed type has no reachable `&mut` path.
   An `Atomic<T>` has no `&mut` path by construction, which is what makes
   `thread.spawn(worker, &counter)` with `pd val counter: AtomicI64` the shared
   counter, and what makes a `datac` with a reachable `var` field the case the
   checker refuses. The rules are structural and builtin (MK9 section 5): there
   is no `Send`/`Sync` marker, because traits are A-12, and each refusal names
   its own reason.

       fun thread.join(handle)

   `join` consumes the handle and returns `Result<T, ThreadErr>`: `Ok` carries
   the value the worker returned, and `Err` carries why it ended without one. A
   panic inside the worker ends that thread and is seen as `Err` by its joiner,
   with the process still alive (MK9 section 2). The main thread keeps today's
   behaviour: a panic in `main` still ends the process.

   A `Thread<T>` is a compiler builtin generic in the shape `Atomic<T>` has: not
   `Copy` (a copy would name one worker twice), and one pointer to a worker
   record the runtime owns, so it is dropped like any other owned value. A handle
   is accepted as a local, a `datac` or `enum` field and a parameter, and refused
   as a `List`/`Map`/`Box`/array element, because joining is a one-shot thing a
   container's own copy-and-drop routines would duplicate.

   A handle nobody joins is dropped by detaching the worker, which keeps running
   while its record is leaked rather than freed under it; a panicking worker's
   frame is abandoned the same way. Both are deliberate and recorded in
   `kelp issue #245` section 5.

   The fence: the module's fence is documented here rather than declared, exactly
   as `std/atomic` documents the atomic methods it declares nowhere: a fence is
   one instruction on either side (`__atomic_thread_fence` in C, `fence seq_cst`
   in IR), so it needs no C body and the compiler lowers it. Its parameter type
   is `std/atomic`'s `Ordering`, named the way any other enum is (`BUG-kQ7Rv` was
   the backend prelude having no typedef for it; it does now).

   The fence has no cell and no receiver. `SeqCst` means one total order over
   every sequentially consistent operation, and a fence is the one operation
   that joins that order while naming no location, which is why it is a free
   function rather than an `Atomic<T>.fence` (MK9 open question 9.2, resolved
   this way by A2). It lives in `std/thread` rather than beside the atomic
   builtin because that is where the thread surface goes.

   A program pays the same `impl "std/atomic"` line every atomic call site pays,
   plus the module qualifier, because that is where `Ordering` is declared:
   `thread.fence(order = atomic.Ordering.SeqCst)`. The qualifier is the enum's,
   not this module's, so nothing here imports it.

   v1 has exactly one ordering, `SeqCst` (MK9 section 3), so `order` has exactly
   one legal value. The argument is the slot that reserves the position for
   `Relaxed`/`Acquire`/`Release`: naming one of those is the ordinary
   unknown-variant error until the variant exists, and adding it widens the enum
   instead of changing this signature.

   A fence is one instruction and changes nothing a single-threaded program can
   observe: `__atomic_thread_fence(__ATOMIC_SEQ_CST)` on the C side and
   `fence seq_cst` on the LLVM side. */

/// Why a joined worker ended without a value, and where: the message carries the
/// panic text, which already names the `.kp` line, so the joiner can report where
/// the worker died (MK9 section 2). One owned `String` field, so the reason is
/// built by the worker's panic and read by whoever joins it.
datac ThreadErr(
    /// The panic text the worker died with, which names the `.kp` line it panicked on.
    val message: String
)

/* MK9-S3-M1 stage C2: `thread.sleep_ms` and `thread.current_id`, the two
   pieces MK6-S2-M1's worker pool needs and `spawn`/`join` alone do not give
   it a worker that polls a queue has to back off between empty pulls, and a
   pool that logs or round-robins by worker wants a cheap, stable way to name
   "which one am I" from inside the running thread.

   Both are ordinary functions, not checker builtins: unlike `spawn`/`join`,
   neither one's type depends on what crosses a thread boundary, so both get
   a normal `.kp` signature and an `extern fun` body, the way `std/time` and
   `std/rand` already work.

   `sleep_ms` is `std/time`'s `sleep_ms` under this module's name. Sleeping
   takes no capability there and it takes none here either the second
   `nanosleep` costs nothing that the first did not already cost, and a
   worker pool wants to spell a backoff as `thread.sleep_ms(n)` without
   importing a namespace named for the wall clock it never reads. */

extern fun sleep_ms_raw(ms: u64)

/// Blocks the calling thread for `ms` milliseconds. It takes no capability.
fun sleep_ms(ms: u64) {
    sleep_ms_raw(ms)
}

/// A stable, cheap id for the calling thread: the main thread and every
/// worker `thread.spawn` starts each get one, and a thread's id never changes
/// across its lifetime. It identifies "which thread is this", not "which Nth
/// worker did I spawn" a detached, unjoined handle's worker still has an id,
/// and nothing in v1 hands out sequential pool slots. Backed by `pthread_self`,
/// which is one instruction and needs no syscall.
extern fun current_id() => u64 = "current_id_raw"
